        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Statue of Liberty</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1875 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Designed by the French sculptor Frédéric Auguste Bartholdi, with Eugène Viollet-le-Duc as first chief engineer and Gustave Eiffel as builder of the framework; paid for by French donors, with the pedestal by Richard Morris Hunt paid for by Americans</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A neoclassical figure of Libertas in repoussé <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> sheets about 2.4 mm thick on an iron framework, Bartholdi having decided on a height of just over 151 feet (46 m), set on a granite-faced concrete pedestal 89 feet (27 m) high</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">On Liberty Island, formerly Bedloe's Island, in Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Bay, part of New York City and of the Statue of Liberty National Monument</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the torch and the tabula ansata inscribed JULY IV MDCCLXXVI; the broken chain at her feet; Emma Lazarus's sonnet The New Colossus; its role as a symbol of welcome to immigrants; Eiffel's curtain wall construction; the 1986 restoration; the green patina of verdigris</span></div>
        </div>
