    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The statue was proposed by the French abolitionist Édouard de Laboulaye as a gift marking American independence and the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>; the French people paid for it and Americans paid for the pedestal.</li><li>Eiffel replaced a planned masonry pier with an iron truss tower, so that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> skin hangs from a framework that lets it move slightly in the wind, an early example of curtain wall construction.</li><li>Built as a symbol of liberty and republican ideals, it came to be seen by arriving immigrants as a sign of welcome, a reading that Emma Lazarus's sonnet of 1883 had anticipated.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Bartholdi considered having Liberty hold a broken chain and decided it would be too divisive after the Civil War, so the statue strides over a chain half-hidden by her robes and difficult to see from the ground.</p></aside>
    </section>

